// error
// #4475 (SPEC §18.3, "`fail e` returns the err result carrying `e`
// (assignable to `E`)"): the `fail` operand was never checked against the
// enclosing function's error type. `vStmt`'s `Tag.FailStmt` arm asked only
// whether the enclosing function was fallible; every other producer of a
// value — `return`, a `let`, a call argument — already routes through
// `vExpectExpr`, so `let e: error = NotAnError{ code: 7 }` has been E0041
// since #2237 while `fail NotAnError{ code: 7 }` was accepted silently and
// segfaulted at run time on the first `e.message()`.
//
// Four rows, one per shape the unchecked path admitted. The first two are
// §14.3's "only a class can be the concrete type behind an interface value"
// (a scalar has nothing to point to); the third is a class that is storable
// but does not satisfy `error`'s single `message(): string`; the fourth is a
// concrete `T!E` whose E is a named class, so a scalar is judged against the
// class directly rather than against an interface.
//
// A checkercase and NOT an `// error` golden under _tests_/cases: that
// directory is selfhost-diffcheck.sh's corpus, where a diagnostic the pinned
// stage0 oracle does not have counts as a FALSEPOS -- 'bit2 rejects code the
// seed accepts', which that differential documents as the one build-breaking
// category and requires to stay 0. Placed here the same program is pinned by
// test-checker-diag, which compares against this .expected rather than
// against a released compiler, so no differential is reddened until the pin
// moves.
class NotAnError {
  code: i64,
}

class MyErr {
  code: i64
  message(): string { return "code=${this.code}" }
}

fn a(): ()! {
  fail "too short"
}

fn b(): ()! {
  fail true
}

fn c(): ()! {
  fail NotAnError{ code: 7 }
}

fn d(): i64!MyErr {
  fail 42
}

fn main() {
  a() catch e {
    print("a\n")
  }
  b() catch e {
    print("b\n")
  }
  c() catch e {
    print("c\n")
  }
  let v = d() catch e {
    0
  }
  print("v=${v}\n")
}
